Q: How left join works with sqlx A: <p>Here's what I got.</p>
<h2>Using Absolute Paths</h2>
<p>I have a struct like so:</p>
<pre><code>type Posts struct {
    Id            string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;posts.id&quot;`
    Uuid          string         `json:&quot;uuid&quot; db:&quot;posts.uuid&quot;`
    Title         string         `json:&quot;title&quot; db:&quot;posts.title&quot;`
    Author        *Users         `json:&quot;author&quot; db:&quot;&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which references <code>Users</code>:</p>
<pre><code>type Users struct {
    Id           string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;users.id&quot;`
    Name         string         `json:&quot;name&quot; db:&quot;users.name&quot;`
    Slug         string         `json:&quot;slug&quot; db:&quot;users.slug&quot;`
    Email        string         `json:&quot;email&quot; db:&quot;users.email&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>We need the table name in the column tags because there are duplicate column names that we will be including in the <code>SELECT</code>.</p>
<p>To make this work, we are also giving sqlx an empty tag for <code>Authors</code> so that it uses the full tag names in <code>Users</code> directly with no traversal.</p>
<h3>Very important to note if you are using MySQL: the column names being returned by <code>go-sql-driver</code> do <strong>not</strong> include the table names (i.e., <code>users.id</code>) unless you enable <a href="https://github.com/go-sql-driver/MYSQL#columnswithalias" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>columnsWithAlias</code></a> in your connection string.</h3>
<p>In my generic query builder function, the columns get specified with the table names for the select and joins (the <code>colName</code> is what's important):</p>
<pre><code>func (q *Query[T]) BuildSelectQuery() (err error) {
    var t T

    var table string

    typeOf := reflect.TypeOf(t).String()
    typeOfSplit := strings.Split(typeOf, &quot;.&quot;)
    if len(typeOfSplit) &gt; 1 {
        table = strings.ToLower(typeOfSplit[1])
    }

    var cols []string
    for _, c := range q.Columns {
        colName := fmt.Sprintf(&quot;%s.%s&quot;, table, c) // table_name.column
        cols = append(cols, colName)
    }
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>This query builder uses reflection to get a table name from the name of the struct.  If we left the table name off from the <code>posts</code> <code>SELECT</code>, we would run into ambiguous column names.</p>
<p>Now execute your query:</p>
<pre><code>err := db.Select(&amp;posts, q.Stmt, q.Args...)
if err != nil {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf(&quot;Error querying Posts rows: %s&quot;, err)
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have no duplicate field names between multiple tables, or if you want to just alias specific columns, you can leave <code>columnsWithAlias</code> off and treat all of the resulting columns as flat column names that will match up to any nested structs with their tags.</p>
<h2>Using Traversal</h2>
<p>In the sqlx documentation there are examples with embedded structs, meaning the fields are top-level in the struct and cannot overlap.  Instead, we create a query response struct with traversal paths that we unpack after the scan, keeping separate the absolute paths of all fields.</p>
<pre><code>type PostsQueryResponse struct {
    Post   *Posts `db:&quot;posts&quot;`
    Author *Users `db:&quot;users&quot;`
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now the column names are normal for the actual data structs:</p>
<pre><code>type Posts struct {
    Id            string         `json:&quot;id&quot; db:&quot;id&quot;`
    Uuid          string         `json:&quot;uuid&quot; db:&quot;uuid&quot;`
    Title         string         `json:&quot;title&quot; db:&quot;title&quot;`
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>This gives it a root struct and paths to follow for <code>posts</code> and <code>users</code>.  If it doesn't have the root struct for the response, it won't know to follow <code>posts</code> for the base table name.</p>
<p>You still need to specify the full table names for the query, and after querying you have to assign each nested <code>Author</code> struct from <code>PostsQueryResponse</code> into its respective <code>Posts</code> struct:</p>
<pre><code>for _, i := range r {
    i.Post.Author = i.Author
    posts = append(posts, *i.Post)
}
</code></pre>
<p>For queries with many potential joins, I kind of like the absolute paths better, but it's good to have options and to understand different ways sqlx is meant to work.</p>
